{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>First.</b> This is \"and if you bring a meal offering of first fruits\" (Leviticus 2:14), for it speaks of the individual, as I shall explain in its place. And it mentioned this after \"you shall not slaughter with leaven the blood of my sacrifice,\" for from there the first fruits begin." "The deficient of understanding say that גדי is derived from מגד, but this is impossible, for mem is not a root letter of מגד. And the reliable witness: עִם פְּרִי מְגָדִים (Song of Songs 4:13), for thus it is never found in the holy tongue. Moreover, what reason [is there for] \"do not boil,\" and no man has the power to boil מגד? And it is written וּמִמֶּגֶד תְּבוּאֹת שָׁמֶשׁ (Deuteronomy 33:14). And what reason to say בחלב, and where do we find such an expression? Moreover, what reason אִמּוֹ — and where is the tree called the mother of the מגד? And all this they thought up because we find this commandment alongside ראשית בכורי אדמתך. But behold, it also appears elsewhere alongside הַבָּשָׂר לֹא תֹאכְלוּ כָּל נְבֵלָה (Deuteronomy 14:21). And that is the straightforward reading; it was simply mentioned alongside the first fruits, for then the kids multiply and grow large.\n\nThere is another [opinion], which says לֹא תְבַשֵּׁל means לֹא תְאַחֵר, that it should not remain with its mother more than seven days. But then what reason to mention this commandment again? Moreover, their proof is בָּשְׁלֵה קָצִיר (Joel 4:13), but it means only boiling, for the sun boils as fire does; and thus הִבְשִׁילוּ אַשְׁכְּלוֹתֶיהָ (Genesis 40:10).\n\nRabbi Shlomo said that גדי is the small, tender one, and this term applies to the young of cattle and sheep. His proof: גְּדִי עִזִּים (Genesis 38:17) — for what need is there to specify? But this is not so, for גדי is never called unless it is of the goats. And in the Arabic tongue it is גדי and is not said of any other species. But there is a difference between גדי and גדי עזים: גדי is larger than גדי עזים, for it still needs to be with the goats; and thus שְׂעִיר and שְׂעִיר עִזִּים.\n\nAnd the Sages received that Israel shall not eat meat in milk. And now I shall explain:\n\nKnow that the Torah's way is to speak of the common practice, as in בַּת הַיַּעֲנָה (Leviticus 11:16). Why did Scripture change to say thus, that \"daughter\" should encompass the whole species — and such we have not found? Know that ostrich meat is dry as wood, and it is not the practice for a person to eat it, for it has no moisture. And of the whole species only the daughter is eaten, for she is female and small and has a little moisture — but not so the small male.\n\nAnd thus no one eats meat in milk, for it is not a palatable food, and the meat will not cook except over a long time, whereas milk is not so. For the custom to this day in the land of Ishmael is that no person eats a lamb cooked in milk, because the lamb has much moisture, and thus in milk it is harmful; therefore it is not eaten. But since kid meat has no moisture, and when it is small it is hot, therefore they cook the kid in milk.\n\nAnd do not wonder that the people of those lands are not accustomed to eat kid of goats, for all physicians agree that there is no meat like it, and they even permitted it to the sick to eat. And so they eat it in Spain and Africa and the land of Israel and Persia and Babylonia. Also thus was the custom of the ancients: שְׁנֵי גְּדָיֵי עִזִּים טוֹבִים (Genesis 27:9); וְנַעֲשֶׂה לְפָנֶיךָ גְּדִי עִזִּים (Judges 13:15); and the priest eats a kid of goats, one for a sin offering.\n\nAnd since it is not the practice of any person to eat raw meat, [one] is forbidden to cook the kid in its mother's milk.\n\nAnd there are those who say that cooking is only by fire — but is it not written וּבָשֵׁל מְבֻשָּׁל בַּמָּיִם (above, 12:9)? And in Chronicles: בִּשְּׁלוּ בַסִּירוֹת (2 Chronicles 35:13).\n\nAnd we have no need to seek the reason for its prohibition, for it is hidden from the eyes of the discerning.\n\nPerhaps it was because it is cruelty of heart to cook the kid in its mother's milk, as in the manner of וְשׁוֹר אוֹ שֶׂה אֹתוֹ וְאֶת בְּנוֹ לֹא תִשְׁחֲטוּ (Leviticus 22:28); also לֹא תִקַּח הָאֵם עַל הַבָּנִים (Deuteronomy 22:6).\n\nAnd the practice of most people, who do not own flocks and buy milk in the market — the milk is gathered from many animals. Perhaps the one who buys the kid does not know who its mother is, and if he bought milk, perhaps the milk contains that of the mother of the kid he bought, and he would transgress. And every doubt that is from the Torah is treated stringently.\n\nAnd behold, even the Ishmaelites acknowledge that if the kid is cooked in its mother's milk — where the offspring is the same [species] — then it is more palatable. And Scripture spoke of the common practice.\n\nAnd the ancients, of blessed memory, were stringent to remove all doubt and forbade meat in milk. And may the Name who gave them wisdom grant them their full reward.\n\nAnd further I shall speak as to why Scripture mentioned this commandment a second time, also a third time, in each of their places."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:38:10.128789095Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}